ion steps, but not a blocker for this scaffold story.\n\n### Findings\n\n| Severity | Summary |\n|----------|---------|\n| — | None. Small, well-typed scope; tests map 1:1 to ACs; no credentials, no runtime logic, no UI. The `.gitignore` addition (`node_modules/`) is a sensible, in-scope addition. |\n\nOne informational note (below finding threshold): `packageManager: \"npm@10\"` is a coarse pin (no minor/patch). The story AC explicitly requires `npm@10`, so this matches the requirement verbatim — not a deviation.\n\n## Review Summary\n\n| Severity | Count | Status |\n|----------|-------|--------|\n| CRITICAL | 0     | pass   |\n| HIGH     | 0     | pass   |\n| MEDIUM   | 0     | pass   |\n| LOW      | 0     | pass   |\n\n**Verdict: APPROVE** — AC1, AC2, AC3 all verified against committed contents; 4/4 unit tests pass; no files modified, nothing committed during this review.","usage":{"total_tokens":22682,"tool_uses":6,"duration_ms":171774},"uuid":"80c82de0-afd9-4af0-a04d-d1bb0ade07dd","session_id":"f8b73ddf-047b-4f01-ba04-11a9c90ca7d2"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9a09c862587d0040","type":"tool_result","content":"<retrieval_status>success</retrieval_status>\n\n<task_id>a11872f4de351a1dc</task_id>\n\n<task_type>local_agent</task_type>\n\n<status>completed</status>\n\n<output>\n\n\n## Review Report — STORY-setup-001a (Review-only pass)\n\n### AC1: Root package.json identity, workspaces, npm@10, Node 22 — PASS\n\n`jq . package.json` confirms every field exactly:\n- `name`: `\"nextmove\"`\n- `private`: `true`\n- `workspaces`: `[\"apps/*\"]`\n- `packageManager`: `\"npm@10\"`\n- `engines.node`: `\"22.x\"`\n- `node -e \"JSON.parse(...)\"` → `valid JSON`\n\n### AC2: turbo 2.x devDependency + turbo-run scripts — PASS\n\n- `devDependencies.turbo`: `\"^2.5.0\"` (satisfies 2.x)\n- `scripts.build`: `\"turbo run build\"`\n- `scripts.test`: `\"turbo run test\"`\n- `scripts.lint`: `\"turbo run lint\"`\n\n### AC3: Scaffold committed to git — PASS\n\n- `git show 6cc5d66 --stat`: commit adds `.gitignore` (+1 line: `node_modules/`), `package-lock.json` (123), `package.json` (13), `test/package.test.cjs` (39).\n- `git ls-files` confirms `package.json` and `test/package.test.cjs` are tracked.\n- `git status --porcelain -- package.json test/package.test.cjs` → empty (exit 0); working tree matches HEAD for both files.\n\n### Test results\n\n`test/package.test.cjs` is a `node:test` file with 4 subtests.\n\n- `node test/package.test.cjs` → **tests 4, pass 4, fail 0, cancelled 0, skipped 0** (all ACs covered: JSON parse/identity, workspaces, npm/engines pins, turbo + scripts).\n- Note: `node --test test/` (directory form) fails on this environment's Node v22.23.2 with `MODULE_NOT_FOUND` — the runner treats the bare directory path as a module entry point. This is an invocation quirk, not a defect in the committed code; running the file directly (or via a glob) works. Worth remembering for future verification steps, but not a blocker for this scaffold story.\n\n### Findings\n\n| Severity | Summary |\n|----------|---------|\n| — | None. Small, well-typed scope; tests map 1:1 to ACs; no credentials, no runtime logic, no UI. The `.gitignore` addition (`node_modules/`) is a sensible, in-scope addition. |\n\nOne informational note (below finding threshold): `packageManager: \"npm@10\"` is a coarse pin (no minor/patch). The story AC explicitly requires `npm@10`, so this matches the requirement verbatim — not a deviation.\n\n## Review Summary\n\n| Severity | Count | Status |\n|----------|-------|--------|\n| CRITICAL | 0     | pass   |\n| HIGH     | 0     | pass   |\n| MEDIUM   | 0     | pass   |\n| LOW      | 0     | pass   |\n\n**Verdict: APPROVE** — AC1, AC2, AC3 all verified against committed contents; 4/4 unit tests pass; no files modified, nothing committed during this review.\n</output>"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f8b73ddf-047b-4f01-ba04-11a9c90ca7d2","uuid":"ebd09167-119f-4a3e-895e-02caac76a75e","timestamp":"2026-08-20T10:07:29.558Z","tool_use_result":{"retrieval_status":"success","task":{"task_id":"a11872f4de351a1dc","task_type":"local_agent","status":"completed","description":"code-reviewer: verify STORY-setup-001a","prompt":"You are a senior code reviewer ensuring high standards of code quality and security.\n\n## Review Process\n\nWhen invoked:\n\n1. **Gather context** — Run `git diff --staged` and `git diff` to see all changes. If no diff, check recent commits with `git log --oneline -5`.\n2. **Understand scope** — Identify which files changed, what feature/fix they relate to, and how they connect.\n3. **Read surrounding code** — Don't review changes in isolation. Read the full file and understand imports, dependencies, and call sites.\n4. **Apply review checklist** — Work through each category below, from CRITICAL to LOW.\n5. **Report findings** — Use the output format below. Only report issues you are confident about (>80% sure it is a real problem).\n\n## Confidence-Based Filtering\n\n**IMPORTANT**: Do not flood the review with noise. Apply these filters:\n\n- **Report** if you are >80% confident it is a real issue\n- **Skip** stylistic preferences unless they violate project conventions\n- **Skip** issues in unchanged code unless they are CRITICAL security issues\n- **Consolidate** similar issues (e.g. \"5 functions missing error handling\" not 5 separate findings)\n- **Prioritize** issues that could cause bugs, security vulnerabilities, or data loss\n\n### Pre-Report Gate\n\nBefore writing a finding, answer all four questions. If any answer is \"no\" or\n\"unsure\", downgrade severity or drop the finding.\n\n1. **Can I cite the exact line?** Name the file and line. Vague findings like\n   \"somewhere in the auth layer\" are not actionable and must be dropped.\n2. **Can I describe the concrete failure mode?** Name the input, state, and bad\n   outcome. If you cannot name the trigger, you are pattern-matching, not\n   reviewing.\n3. **Have I read the surrounding context?** Check callers, imports, and tests.\n   Many apparent issues are already handled one frame up or guarded by a type.\n4. **Is the severity defensible?** A missing JSDoc is never HIGH. A single\n   `any` in a test fixture is never CRITICAL. Severity inflation erodes trust\n   faster than missed findings.\n\n### HIGH / CRITICAL Require Proof\n\nFor any finding tagged HIGH or CRITICAL, include:\n- The exact snippet and line number\n- The specific failure scenario: input, state, and outcome\n- Why existing guards, such as types, validation, or framework defaults, do not\n  catch it\n\nIf you cannot produce all three, demote to MEDIUM or drop.\n\n### It Is Acceptable And Expected To Return Zero Findings\n\nA clean review is a valid review. Do not manufacture findings to justify the\ninvocation. If the diff is small, well-typed, tested, and follows the project's\npatterns, the correct output is a summary with zero rows and verdict `APPROVE`.\n\nManufactured findings, filler nits, speculative \"consider using X\", and\nhypothetical edge cases without a trigger are the primary failure mode of LLM\nreviewers and directly undermine this agent's usefulness.\n\n## Common False Positives - Skip These\n\nPatterns that LLM reviewers commonly mis-flag. Skip unless you have evidence\nspecific to this codebase:\n\n- **\"Consider adding error handling\"** on a call whose error path is handled by\n  the caller or framework.\n- **\"Missing input validation\"** when the function is internal and its callers\n  already validate.\n- **\"Magic number\"** for well-known constants.\n- **\"Function too long\"** for exhaustive `switch` statements, configuration\n  objects, test tables, or generated code.\n- **\"Missing JSDoc\"** on single-purpose internal helpers.\n- **\"Prefer `const` over `let`\"** when the variable is reassigned.\n- **\"Possible null dereference\"** when the preceding line narrows the type.\n- **\"Should use TypeScript\"** or **\"Should have types\"** in a JavaScript-only\n  file.\n- **\"Hardcoded value\"** for values in test fixtures.\n- **Security theater**: flagging non-cryptographic `Math.random()` or `eval` in\n  an explicit code-loading surface.\n\nWhen tempted to flag one of the above, ask: \"Would a senior engineer on this\nteam actually change it in review?\" If no, skip.\n\n## Review Checklist\n\n### Security (CRITICAL)\nHardcoded credentials, SQL injection, XSS, path traversal, CSRF, auth bypasses,\ninsecure dependencies, exposed secrets in logs.\n\n### Code Quality (HIGH)\nLarge functions (>50 lines), large files (>800 lines), deep nesting (>4),\nmissing error handling, mutation patterns, console.log statements, missing\ntests, dead code.\n\n### Performance (MEDIUM)\nInefficient algorithms, unnecessary re-renders, missing caching, synchronous I/O.\n\n### Best Practices (LOW)\nTODO/FIXME without tickets, missing JSDoc for public APIs, poor naming,\nmagic numbers, inconsistent formatting.\n\n## Review Output Format\n\nOrganize findings by severity. For each issue:\n\n```\n[SEVERITY] Summary\nFile: path:line\nIssue: description\nFix: suggestion\n```\n\n### Summary Format\n\nEnd every review with:\n\n```\n## Review Summary\n\n| Severity | Count | Status |\n|----------|-------|--------|\n| CRITICAL | 0     | pass   |\n| HIGH     | 0     | pass   |\n| MEDIUM   | 0     | pass   |\n| LOW      | 0     | pass   |\n\nVerdict: APPROVE | WARNING | BLOCK\n```\n\n## Approval Criteria\n\n- **Approve**: No CRITICAL or HIGH issues, including clean reviews with zero findings.\n- **Warning**: HIGH issues only (can merge with caution)\n- **Block**: CRITICAL issues found — must fix before merge\n\nDo not withhold approval to appear rigorous. If the diff is clean, approve it.\n\n## v1.8 AI-Generated Code Review Addendum\n\nWhen reviewing AI-generated changes, prioritize:\n1. Behavioral regressions and edge-case handling\n2. Security assumptions and trust boundaries\n3. Hidden coupling or accidental architecture drift\n4. Unnecessary complexity\n\n---\n\n## YOUR TASK\n\nWorkspace: /home/nitin-sharma/ralph-workspaces/6896163a-932b-4b96-bfe9-cdbb022c3538/c7df6267-784a-460a-addd-6d781a21b6f5\n\nRun `cd \"/home/nitin-sharma/ralph-workspaces/6896163a-932b-4b96-bfe9-cdbb022c3538/c7df6267-784a-460a-addd-6d781a21b6f5\"` first for all commands.\n\nThis is a REVIEW-ONLY pass for story **STORY-setup-001a** (\"Create Root NextMove package.json With Workspaces\"). The implementation was committed in commit `6cc5d66` (\"chore: add root package.json with npm workspaces, npm@10, Node 22 engines, turbo 2.x, and turbo-run scripts\"). This story is a pure scaffold/config story — no UI, no runtime services.\n\nVerify each acceptance criterion by reading the ACTUAL committed file contents and running checks (do not assume):\n\nAC1: Root package.json exists with name `nextmove`, `private: true`, workspaces array containing `apps/*`, `packageManager` pinned to `npm@10`, and `engines.node` `22.x`.\n- Check: `jq . package.json` — confirm every field exactly.\n\nAC2: devDep